  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🤼 Wrestling Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Ring names for heroes and villains, monsters, high-flyers, tag teams and funny wrestlers, all new."
    },
    "intro": "A ring name has to work when an announcer bellows it across an arena, and it should tell the crowd whether to cheer or boo. This **wrestling name generator** makes ring names for heroes and villains, comedy acts, monsters, high-flyers, tag teams and female wrestlers, or builds one from your own first name. It's independent and isn't connected to WWE, AEW or any other promotion, and the names of real wrestlers are left out.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good wrestling names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, one from each style:</p><ul><li><strong>Kid Valour</strong>: a young hero the crowd wants to see win.</li><li><strong>Lord Vexley</strong>: a posh title and a sneery surname, so the boos start before the music ends.</li><li><strong>Tarquin the Rule-Breaker</strong>: a villain who tells you exactly what he plans to do.</li><li><strong>Custard Cream Barry</strong>: a comedy wrestler who would rather be having a cup of tea.</li><li><strong>Gravelgut the Unmovable</strong>: a monster name that sounds like it weighs a tonne.</li><li><strong>El Colibrí Veloz</strong>: Spanish for \"the swift hummingbird\", for a masked high-flyer.</li><li><strong>The Thunderfield Brothers</strong>: a tag team that sounds like family, even if they aren't.</li><li><strong>The Duchess of Dropkicks</strong>: a title for a woman who rules the ring.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How ring names work",
        "content": "<p>In wrestling, the hero is the <strong>face</strong> (from \"babyface\") and the villain is the <strong>heel</strong>. A ring name is the first clue to which is which. Faces get short, bright, friendly names: a nickname and a first name, or \"Kid\" with a word like Courage or Comet. Heels get titles, sneers and long posh names, because a crowd loves to boo someone who thinks he is better than them.</p><p>Most ring names follow a few patterns, and the generator uses them all: nickname and first name (Fearless Leo), first name and strong surname (Ruby Stormfield), title and surname (Baron Fenwick), and the single huge word for a monster (Gravelgut). In Mexican lucha libre, masked luchadores often take a Spanish name for an animal, a star or the weather, which is where El Cometa Azul and La Tormenta Veloz come from.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Tag teams, comedy acts and the British style",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Tag teams</strong> need a name that works for two people at once. \"The\" with a plural (The Copper Hounds), a shared surname (The Thunderfield Brothers) or a pair of things that belong together (Salt &amp; Vinegar) all work.</li><li><strong>Comedy wrestlers</strong> get their laughs from things that don't belong in a ring: an ordinary job (The Masked Crossword Setter), a snack (Bourbon Biscuit Gary) or a gentle title on a painful move (Grandad Suplex).</li><li><strong>British wrestling</strong> had its own golden age on ITV's <em>World of Sport</em> on Saturday afternoons, with big characters like Big Daddy and Giant Haystacks. Those names and the famous ones from American and Japanese wrestling are all left out of the generator.</li></ul>"
